## Best Practices for Data Organization {#sec-data-best-practices}

### Naming Conventions {#sec-tidy-naming-conventions}

Good names are the cheapest documentation you will ever write, and the shell, R, and Python all reward the same habits. A file name should say what the file contains and, where it matters, when: `patient_outcomes_2024-03-15.csv` tells you more than `data_final_v2.csv` ever will, and a date written in ISO 8601 order (`YYYY-MM-DD`) sorts chronologically when the files are listed alphabetically. Avoid spaces, which every command-line tool treats as a separator between arguments, and avoid characters with special meanings in the shell or in URLs such as `& ? * / \ : ( ) ' "`; underscores and hyphens are the safe separators. Remember that Unix file systems are case-sensitive, so `Data.csv` and `data.csv` are different files; pick one convention (lower case is the usual choice) and keep to it.

Column names follow the same logic with one extra constraint: they become variable names inside your code. Lower case with underscores (`sample_id`, `gene_expression`) can be typed without back-ticks or brackets in every language we use, a name should be meaningful (`treatment_group`, not `tg`), and it should start with a letter, because a name beginning with a digit is not a legal identifier in R or Python. The course dataset's fourth column, `Geno&Micro`, is a small illustration of what happens otherwise: it has to be quoted or renamed in every tool that touches it (@sec-data-wrangling).

### File Formats {#sec-file-formats}

@tbl-file-formats lists the formats you will meet most often; @sec-appendix-data-formats describes them in more detail.

| Format | Extension | Best For |
|:-------|:----------|:---------|
| Comma-separated | `.csv` | General tabular data |
| Tab-separated | `.tsv` | Data containing commas |
| Plain text | `.txt` | Simple data, scripts |
| Excel | `.xlsx` | Sharing with non-programmers |
| RDS | `.rds` | R-specific data with types preserved |
| Parquet | `.parquet` | Large datasets, fast reading |

: Common data file formats {#tbl-file-formats}

For long-term storage and sharing, prefer plain text formats (CSV, TSV) over proprietary formats. They're human-readable and don't require specific software.

### Documentation {#sec-documentation}

A data file is only as useful as the description that travels with it. The standard form is a **data dictionary**, sometimes called a codebook: a second, small table with one row per variable that records the variable's name, what it measures, its units, the values or categories it may take, how missing values are coded, where the data came from, and when the file was created or last changed. It need not be elaborate; a `README.md` or a `variables.csv` next to the data is enough. The `Data_Folder/README.md` that accompanies the course dataset (@sec-course-dataset) is an example. Write it while you still remember what the columns mean, because six months later you will not, and neither will the collaborator who inherits the project.

### Preserving Raw Data {#sec-preserving-raw-data}

::: {.callout-warning title="Never Modify Raw Data"}
Always keep an unchanged copy of your original data. Create a separate processed version for analysis. This allows you to:

- Retrace your steps if something goes wrong
- Rerun analysis with different preprocessing
- Share the original data with collaborators

Keep raw files in their own directory, with write permission removed if possible (`chmod a-w`, @sec-chmod). All cleaning and transformation should be done programmatically, in scripts that can be re-run, with the outputs saved to new files.
:::

### Keeping a Data File Clean {#sec-data-file-dos-donts}

The naming rules above cover file and column names; a few further habits keep the *contents* of a data file usable whether you edit it with Unix tools, R, Python, or a spreadsheet program. Keep the file rectangular: a new observation is a new row and a new variable is a new column, and there is exactly one header row, with concise but descriptive variable names. Write dates in ISO 8601 form, which sorts correctly as text and contains no delimiter characters. Represent missing values the same way everywhere, either as empty cells or as `NA`, never as a scattering of `-`, `N/A`, `missing`, and `?`. And keep the data dictionary from @sec-documentation alongside the file so that the two stay in step.

The mistakes to avoid are mostly ways of smuggling information into places where software cannot see it. Do not mix types within a column: if a column holds numbers, every entry is a number or an explicit `NA`, not a note such as "below detection", which turns the whole column into text. Do not use the file's delimiter inside a value, because in a comma-separated file `March 8, 2024` becomes two fields. Do not paste from formatted documents such as Word, whose invisible formatting characters can corrupt a file, and do not open a data file in Excel to edit it unless you know what it will silently convert, as the gene-name story in @sec-excel-genes shows. Do not encode information in colour, bold, or cell borders, because formatting is lost the moment the file is exported; put the information in a column instead. And do not leave empty rows or columns as visual separators, since they break the rectangular structure that every import function expects.

::: {.callout-tip title="The one-line version"}
One header row, one type per column, one way of writing missing values, no formatting-as-data, and a data dictionary next to the file.
:::

## Data Types {#sec-tidy-data-types}

Understanding data types helps you organize and analyze correctly.

### Categorical vs. Quantitative {#sec-categorical-vs-quantitative}


::: {#tbl-data-types-table .cell layout-align="center" tbl-cap='Classification of data types'}

```{.r .cell-code}
data_types <- tibble(
  Category = c("Categorical", "Categorical", "Quantitative", "Quantitative"),
  Type = c("Ordinal", "Nominal", "Ratio", "Interval"),
  Description = c(
    "Ordered categories",
    "Unordered categories",
    "True zero, ratios meaningful",
    "No true zero"
  ),
  Examples = c(
    "small, medium, large",
    "red, blue, green",
    "height, weight, concentration",
    "temperature (°C), calendar year"
  )
)

data_types |>
  gt() |>
  tab_header(title = "Data Type Classification")
```

